## 1. Background, Context and Motivation

Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) systems are widely used to automate software testing and increasingly also to execute performance benchmarks. By incorporating performance testing into CI pipelines, software projects can continuously monitor performance and identify regressions introduced by changes to the codebase.

Reliable performance regression detection, however, depends on benchmark results being sufficiently reproducible and comparable between executions. This becomes challenging when benchmarks are executed in heterogeneous or partially unknown environments, such as cloud-hosted CI runners. Platforms such as GitHub Actions may execute jobs on different underlying machines, and differences in processor architecture, processor generation, available resources, virtualization, system load, and other environmental factors can influence benchmark results independently of changes to the software being tested.

Consequently, a difference between two benchmark results does not necessarily represent a software performance regression. It may instead originate from differences in the infrastructure on which the benchmarks were executed.

A straightforward approach is to rerun both the current and previous software versions on the same hardware. However, repeatedly executing historical versions increases CI execution time and computational cost and may not always be practical. An alternative would be to compensate for differences between execution environments, allowing newly obtained benchmark results to be compared with historical measurements without rerunning the corresponding historical commits.

This thesis investigates whether such comparisons can be made sufficiently reliable through normalization, reference benchmarks, statistical techniques, or related methods for accounting for hardware-induced performance variation.

### Application domain

The experimental evaluation will be delimited to **numerical-analysis and scientific-computing applications**.

The benchmarking and experimental infrastructure itself will be implemented primarily in **Python**. Python will be used to orchestrate benchmark executions, interact with the CI/CD environment, collect and process benchmark results, and perform statistical analysis.

