## 3. Approach and Methodology

The thesis will combine a literature study with an experimental evaluation using numerical-analysis and scientific-computing workloads.

### Literature study

A literature review will be conducted covering relevant research and established practices within areas including:

* performance benchmarking of numerical and scientific-computing software.
* performance regression detection;
* benchmark reproducibility and variability;
* performance measurement in virtualized and cloud environments;
* statistical methods for detecting performance changes;
* benchmark normalization and calibration techniques;

The literature review will establish the scientific foundation of the thesis and identify existing methods that may be applicable to heterogeneous CI environments.

### Experimental study

An experimental benchmarking environment will be developed in which numerical algorithms can be executed under different hardware and/or CI runner configurations.

The applications being benchmarked will be implemented as **compiled C++ programs**, with the selected workloads representing computationally relevant numerical-analysis tasks. Candidate workloads may include numerical linear algebra, numerical integration, iterative algorithms, or other computationally intensive numerical algorithms, depending on their suitability for reproducible benchmarking.

The **benchmarking and analysis infrastructure will be implemented in Python**. It will be responsible for orchestrating benchmark executions, collecting measurements, recording relevant execution-environment information, and analysing the resulting data.

Repeated benchmark executions will be used to characterize both within-environment and between-environment variation. The experiments will investigate how much measured performance changes when the software and algorithm remain unchanged but the execution environment differs.

Based on the literature review and initial experiments, one or more approaches for compensating for environmental variation will be implemented and evaluated. Candidate approaches may include:

* reference or calibration benchmarks executed alongside the target benchmark;
* relative performance measurements;
* normalization based on characteristics of the execution environment;
* statistical modelling of benchmark variation;
* statistical change or regression detection techniques.

Controlled software performance changes may be introduced to evaluate whether the investigated methods can correctly distinguish actual software regressions from infrastructure-related variation.

The approaches will be compared using appropriate measures such as detection accuracy, false-positive and false-negative rates, sensitivity to regression magnitude, and stability across execution environments.

The final methodology and experimental design may be refined based on findings from the literature review and preliminary experiments.

