## 5. Expected Contribution to Knowledge

The thesis is expected to contribute empirical knowledge about the impact of heterogeneous execution environments on automated performance benchmarking of numerical-analysis software.

More specifically, the work aims to provide:

* an empirical characterization of benchmark variability across different CI execution environments;
* an evaluation of methods for compensating for hardware- and infrastructure-induced performance variation;
* an assessment of the circumstances under which historical benchmark measurements can be compared with new measurements without rerunning historical software revisions;
* an analysis of the limitations and trade-offs associated with the investigated approaches;
* practical guidance for applying performance regression detection to compiled numerical and scientific-computing software in heterogeneous CI/CD environments.

Where appropriate, the thesis will also produce a prototype implementation consisting of a Python-based benchmarking and analysis framework integrated with a CI/CD environment and C++ numerical applications.

The intended contribution is therefore not only a technical implementation but an experimentally supported evaluation of whether, and under which conditions, reliable performance regression detection across heterogeneous CI environments is feasible.

The numerical-analysis application domain provides a controlled and technically meaningful context for the evaluation, while the underlying findings may provide insights relevant to other computationally intensive software domains.

